-- title:
--   Kernel of the first node residue is (varpi, x)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$, a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, a level $N \neq 0$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $q$, a ring homomorphism $red : A \to k$, modular polynomial data for $q$ satisfying the Kronecker congruence, integrality hypotheses for the two Hecke maps $\bar\alpha, \bar\beta$ at level $N$ and prime $q$, and a place specialization $P$ for these data; let $R$ be a prolongation tuple over $P$, so in particular $R$ carries two regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-level-$Nq$ modular functions together with reduction maps to the characteristic-$q$ modular function field. Assume $q \nmid N$; that $R$ is a model (the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws); the order law at the affine places fixed by the square of the geometric Frobenius on places; a finite set $W$ of places of $\mathrm{modularFunctionFieldC}\ k\ N$, each supersingular (rational, affine, with $j$-value in the supersingular set); the regularity law and the node-value law for $W$. Let $K \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be a finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$, let $w \in W$, and assume every $a \in k$ with $a^{q^2} = a$ lies in the image of $A \cap K \to k$ induced by $red$. Let $c = (x,y)$ be node coordinates over $K$ at $w$: elements of the node ring $S = R.\mathrm{nodeIntegersOver}\ K\ w$ (those level-$Nq$ functions integral for $R_1$, for $R_2$ and at every place of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ above $w$ on the first component, with Laurent expansion in the field generated over $K$) with first residue of $x$ zero and second residue of order $1$ at the arithmetic-Frobenius translate of $w$, and second residue of $y$ zero and first residue of order $1$ at $w$. Let $\varpi \in A \cap K$ be such that an element of $A \cap K$ reduces to $0$ in $k$ exactly when it is a multiple of $\varpi$. Then for $g \in S$ the first node residue of $g$ at $w$ vanishes if and only if $g$ lies in the ideal of $S$ generated by the constant function $\varpi$ and by $x$.
--
--   This is the local description, at a supersingular point of the mod-$q$ fibre, of the kernel of the reduction of the $K$-rational node ring to its first branch: the kernel is cut out by the uniformiser of $A \cap K$ together with the branch equation $x$, as for a normal crossing in the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_0(Nq)$. It is used by the refinement of the same statement in which the vanishing condition is replaced by a lower bound on the order of the first residue.
